# THE INCIDENCE RANK AND CYCLE SPACE OF EVERY GRAPH THIS MODEL COMPOSES. One row per graph: nodes,
# edges, components, and the two dimensions that follow.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ `rank = n - c` AND `cycle space = m - n + c`, WHICH IS THE PARAMETER THE THREE GRAPHS
#    DIFFER ON. A graph with cycles has NO ORDINAL rank and ALWAYS has a MATRIX rank, and
#    confusing the two produces a wrong claim: that a cyclic graph has no rank
#    at all. It has one. **A well-founded relation is simply one whose cycle space is ZERO**, and
#    the ordinal rank is what you get for free when it is.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE CYCLE SPACE IS WHERE A WEIGHTED GRAPH'S RULE LIVES. An edge vector decomposes into
#    the ROW SPACE, a gradient, plus the LEFT NULLSPACE, the cycle part.
#    `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` asks that the cycle component of `log phi` vanish,
#    which says `log phi` is a gradient, which says a POTENTIAL exists: an absolute log-size per
#    unit whose differences are the filed factors. A rule written as a walk round a loop IS a
#    subspace condition. Gilbert Strang's graph chapter is the reference.
#
# ⛔ COUNTS ARE NOT WRITTEN IN THIS HEADER, BECAUSE THIS RELATION IS WHAT PRINTS THEM. Run it.
#   The corpus moves and a sentence does not.
#
# ⚠️ COMPONENTS ARE UNDIRECTED, because `n - c` and `m - n + c` are facts about the underlying
#   undirected graph. Reading them off the directed edges gives a different and wrong `c`.
# ⭐⭐⭐ AND IT IS NOW ONE SCOPE OF `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` RATHER THAN A SECOND COPY OF THE
#    WALK. This file and `rank/decomposition.sqlc` each carried `nodes`, `sym`, `reach` and
#    `comp`, twenty lines of the same undirected component search differing by one column, and
#    the other file's `filing IS NULL` rows were exactly these rows. ⛔ One file was the other
#    restricted, and nothing said so, so a fix to either walk had to be remembered twice.
#
# ⚠️ THE PIN IS `filing IS NULL` AND THE COLUMN IS PROJECTED AWAY, which is the shape rule 9
#   warns about. It is declared here and in the `--` line because it is the WHOLE graph rather
#   than a slice of one: the per-filing cut is a different question and `rank/decomposition.sqlc`
#   is the file that asks it.
-- rank/graph_measures.sqlc at the corpus-wide scope: the two graphs as incidence matrices.
SELECT m.graph, m.n_nodes, m.m_edges, m.c_components, m.cycle_space_dim, m.rank_of_incidence
FROM (
    :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
) m
WHERE m.filing IS NULL
